f many of the leading  data science techniques  from back when random force decision  trees kind of took over  to light gbms xg boost  in the introduction of libraries like  paris  so what better community to introduce  great technologies like tensorflow and  tpus  than through capital competitions the  community is filled with data scientists  who are  eager to experiment and quickly uncover  the benefits  and shortcomings of newly introduced  products  and methodologies and the kaggle  competition can provide a structured and  engaging format  to test them out and specifically for  tpus and tensorflow  our competition platform allows us to  host really large data sets  and offer both time and resource  constrained formats  that can help truly capture the power  and understand the benefits  of these particular tools it's about to  check out the latest tensorflow edition  calling it 2.x as they continue to be  updated on the  platform especially with gpu or tpu  kaggle makes it easy  you can see from the list on this slide  that we've eliminated a lot of the  obstacles to making tensorflow 2.x  build your models fast and with with  next to no specialized code  no provisioning of the right kind of vm  no setup  we already have data sets that are ready  to go our cloud notebook platform  removes the burden of needing to  reconcile versions or libraries or  packages  across collaborators so sharing and  iterating the is a breeze  and finally we even provide a weekly  allotment to tpu and gpu time  at no cost to our users all over the  world  the integration benefits afforded to  tensorflow also apply to tpus  they're directly accessible from our  kaggle notebooks without any additional  setup  we have generous data sets that are  accessible via google cloud storage that  allow for efficient data access  30 hours of tpu use per week are granted  to each user  we've even hosted five competitions just  in the last six months  aimed at these particular tools most  recently we hosted a competition  that was tpu-centric with the google  team jigsaw  that was seeking to evaluate and  classify toxic comments on wikipedia  we also just launched a competition  called contradictory my dear watson  which seeks to use tpus and natural  language processing to evaluate grammar  but today we want to look specifically  at our first two competitions  introduced cpus to over 5 million data  scientists  first being tensorflow 2.0 question  answering and the second being one on  flower classification  now tensorflow is already very heavily  used in our competitions  it's currently being used in all of our  running competitions but  in the past few months we've run two  tensorflow competitions that i mentioned  this one on top being a question  answering nlp  competition built around the launch of  tensorflow 2.0  and a new competition for image  classification that introduced  tpus for the first time to our platform  we already have tens of thousands of  people using tensorflow 2.x  right now to solve problems and three of  the top  solutions in the question answering  competition ended up using tensorflow  and tpus and so next we want to do is  let's dive into some of the code  let's look at some of the code right now  that will build a model on kaggle  for our competition to classify flowers  on cpu  gpu and tpu with no changes so phil  i'm going to hand it over to you to walk  through some of the cochlear use  competitions  thanks addison we start off simple by  importing tensorflow 2  and the kaggle datasets library the rest  of the code here does a bit of magic  it figures out what kind of accelerator  is attached to your vm and allows us to  automatically parallelize your model  building  this will return an appropriate strategy  whether using a v3 tpu  or a simple cpu vm basically this code  asks for a tpu and if it gets one it  connects to it and sets it up  if it can't get a tpu it sets that up as  well the rest of the code in these  slides 