r  predictions into the right form  and write our predictions out in the  format that kaggle can read with a few  tweaks  and a few more epochs this model can  actually score pretty well  i'll finish the code section with some  of the fun flowers that our model has  made predictions on  you can see that some are right they've  got a little okay above them and some  are wrong if you're interested you're  more than welcome to drop by the pedals  to the metal competition  and beat these results that won't be  hard but it will probably be fun  and now addison back to you  thanks phil uh i still demonstrated in  his code review using tpus on kaggle  it's a super easy way  um to get used to tpus in the flower  competition assume the flower  classification competition  that phil highlighted we had over 24 000  tpu using sessions by kaggle users and a  very small amount needed  any amount of help to get started thanks  to some getting started work  done by some members of the team over 80  percent of users needed no help to begin  using tpus  as you can see in this screenshot of top  kaggle notebooks in the flower  classification competition  users quickly created and shared their  own tpu based notebooks  and began to train each other with  discussion topics and highly uploaded  notebooks  many of these top notebooks came from  kaggle's highest ranked  grand master users it's really exciting  to see data science community very  quickly learning  themselves so they can then quickly  teach others  so let's highlight some very specific  outcomes this competition in a kaggle  notebook  users can elect to use a cpu gpu or tpu  like phil mentioned  so we pick two of the top performing  models using each tpus  and gpus they each use effectively the  same code  the tpu model trained on 25 000 images  in an hour and 15 minutes and that same  model would run out of memory  when they try to flip it over gpus the  comparative gpu model  took five hours to train those same 25  000 images  but they had to be able to reduce size  in the end the accuracy still didn't  prove competitive with the tpu model  now prior to this competition there have  been some slight murmurings of tv use  but once the first competition was  launched in february of this year you  can see the large  jump in discussion four mentions per  month about tpus  and it continued to rise now as we  introduce more competitions  including a competition designed to help  introduce tpus to the uninitiated  we're looking forward to seeing how not  just discussion of tpus and tensorflow  continuity increase  but how the data science community  continues to find great insights  in their spread so if you're interested  in seeing tensorflow 2 at work on kaggle  come check it out try out the code that  phil just showed you  compete in the flower competition or try  it out with a data set of your choice  we're traveling with our past  competitions or on our hosted notebook  platform where we give you  some tpu hours to play with in the  coming months we'll be launching  even more competitions so you can  leverage the power of tensorflow  and tpus on capital now it's been a big  group  effort i want to give a special thanks  to martin garner who is the devrel  program manager project manager who led  the effort to integrate tpus  so thanks so much to him for his hard  work it's pretty awesome to see how easy  it is to use tips for flow and kaggle  right now and so finally thanks to you  for joining us with our session today  thank you for your time  please come visit us over at the dory  we'll be hanging out all week  answering any questions you may have  about tpus and tensorflow on cattle  and again thanks so much have a great  day

